text:
I've got an 'cused wife, she always be complainin',

An her mother to keep ;

She says that she can't do nothin',

Not even fetch up water;

But what if she couldn't?

For in the end we were all of us enslaved;
our bodies racked with whips; our limbs dismembered;
and forced to wander from place to place in exile
for years together.

The Sun and Moon are joined : now for one hour

They join together and then part again ;
Yet have they not each other thus outworn —

As lovers oft, when, after a year's pain,
They meet, and kiss and say " I will be thine,"

But soon forget, and both pursue their way;
No, 'twixt them there is no such change of place ;

Each feels the other's presence — knows how near.

A third poem from the same hand :

For this, too, we must thank thee, Sun
